    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my sociology thesis and I'm getting a bit bogged down in the results section. Could you explain the practical difference between a p-value and a confidence interval? Specifically, if I have a p-value of 0.04 and a 95% confidence interval that doesn't include zero for my coefficient, how should I describe the significance of these findings in a formal paper?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While both relate to statistical significance, the p-value tells you the probability of observing your data if there were no real effect (a null hypothesis of zero). In contrast, a 95% confidence interval provides a range of plausible values for the true effect. Seeing a p-value of 0.04 with a non-zero CI suggests your finding is statistically significant at the $\\alpha = 0.05$ level, and you can formally state that the effect is significantly different from zero. This indicates a reliable estimate of the true relationship exists within that calculated interval.",
      "source": "target",
