    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So even if the effect size is strong, there's still that underlying uncertainty from the sampling error. It almost feels like the noise is something intrinsically unpredictable that just exists within the data itself, making it hard to ever be 100% certain. Is there a specific way to describe that \"unpredictable\" element in my thesis so it doesn't sound like my study is just flawed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can frame this uncertainty by discussing the concept of sampling error inherent in any empirical study. Instead of suggesting the study is flawed, frame it as acknowledging the limitations of probabilistic inference, such as \"Our results provide a statistically significant estimate, though this estimate is subject to inherent sampling error.\" Furthermore, you can discuss the generalizability of your findings, noting that the observed effect is probable for the population, but not guaranteed with absolute certainty.",
